TRANSFORMER PUZZLES
===================

  My solutions to Sasha Rush's Transformer Puzzles.

      https://github.com/srush/Transformer-Puzzles

  Short coding puzzles based on the internals of the Transformer.
  Based on Thinking Like Transformers (Weiss, Goldberg, Yahav) and
  the RASPy blog post.

  The goal is to build a Transformer that does elementary-school
  addition — e.g. turn "19492+23919" into the correct sum.


MODEL
-----

  Each exercise is a function from seq to seq. Length is fixed;
  everything runs in parallel over the sequence. A global indices
  gives each position. where branches per position, as in NumPy.

  The main tool is attention: build a selector from key and query,
  combine selectors with logical ops, then aggregate with
  .value(...). Earlier puzzle functions may be reused.

  Bodies below are the function body only, including return.


SOLUTIONS
---------

  0  head

      return (key(indices) == query(0)).value(seq)

  1  index

      return (key(indices) == query(i)).value(seq)

  2  shift

      return (key(indices) == query(indices - i)).value(seq, default=default)

  3  ralign

      has_non_pad_after = ((query("_") != key(seq)) & (key(indices) > query(indices))).value(1)
      cut = ((key(has_non_pad_after) == query(0)) & (query("_") == key(seq))).value(1)
      return shift(cut, ldefault, seq)

  4  split

      i = (query(v) == key(seq)).value(indices)
      blank = "0" if not get_first_part else "_"
      x = where((i > indices) if get_first_part else (i < indices), seq, blank)
      return ralign("0", x)

  5  minimum

      smaller = (key(seq) < query(seq)).value(1)
      tiebreak = ((key(seq) == query(seq)) & (key(indices) < query(indices))).value(1)
      return ((key(smaller) == query(0)) & (key(tiebreak) == query(0))).value(seq)

  6  first

      tiebreak = ((key(seq) == query(token)) & (key(indices) < query(indices))).value(1)
      return ((key(seq) == query(token)) & (key(tiebreak) == query(0))).value(indices)

  7  slide

      nn = ((key(seq) != query("<")) & (key(indices) >= query(indices))).value(1)
      return ((key(seq) != query("<")) & (key(nn) == query(nn))).value(seq)
